<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liguria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Liguria is a region of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> along the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, between the Maritime <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and the sea, with its capital at Genoa. It takes its name from the ancient Ligures, whose lands Augustus organised as Regio IX of Italy. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> its coast, the Riviera, was dominated by the Republic of Genoa, and it passed to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liguria.jpg" alt="Liguria" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Liguria_in_Italy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
